/-!
# The regular integer model at the generic prime

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PJ, case (3), condition (4) of
lemma-J-2 at the zero prime; Fields, Tag 09HE
(blueprint: lem-trf-integers-generic-regular-model).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace Int

/-- The image of the integers in a finite purely inseparable extension of `ℚ` is the
regular finite integer model. The algebra structure represents the given embedding `ι`;
the integer cast is its composite with `ℤ → ℚ`. Bijectivity below is for the canonical
map `ℤ → B`, not merely an unspecified isomorphism of rings. The `IsRegularRing` clause
includes regular locality at every prime, and `IsRegularScheme` includes local Noetherianity.
The regular spectrum is the whole open subset of itself.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PJ, proof of case (3), condition (4)
of lemma-J-2 at the zero prime; Fields, Tag 09HE; Algebra, Tags 034X, 00PD, 00KU, 00OD
(blueprint: lem-trf-integers-generic-regular-model).

Proof:
1. By the characteristic-zero convention for purely inseparable extensions (09HE),
   `ι : ℚ → L` is surjective. A field homomorphism is injective: a nonzero kernel
   element would give `1 = 0` by multiplication by its inverse. Thus the integer
   composite is injective, and its corestriction to its image `B` is a ring isomorphism.
2. Every element of `B` is the image of an integer, hence an integer multiple of `1`.
   Thus `1` generates `B` as a finite integer module. Lift `x : L` to a rational `a/d`
   with `d ≠ 0`; its image is the quotient of the images of `a` and `d`, and the latter
   is nonzero by injectivity.
3. `lem-weil-pbc-z-localizations-dvr` gives Noetherianity of `ℤ`. Transport a finite
   generating set of the inverse image of each ideal along the isomorphism `ℤ ≃ B`:
   applying the isomorphism to its linear-combination formula generates the ideal of `B`.
4. For each prime `q` of `B`, its inverse image `P` in `ℤ` is prime. The fraction maps
   `a/s ↦ e(a)/e(s)` and `b/t ↦ e⁻¹(b)/e⁻¹(t)` are inverse localization isomorphisms;
   denominators avoid the corresponding primes and the fraction relations are preserved.
   Image and inverse image give inverse ideal correspondences preserving inclusion,
   primality and generating sets, so they preserve Noetherianity, locality, Krull
   dimension and the number of generators of the maximal ideal, hence regular locality.
5. By `lem-weil-pbc-z-localizations-dvr`, either `P = (0)` and its localization is `ℚ`,
   regular local by `lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring`, or `P = (p)` for a rational prime.
   In the latter case the localization is Noetherian local of dimension one, with
   maximal ideal generated by `p/1`. This generator is nonzero: otherwise `up = 0`
   for an integer `u ∉ (p)`, impossible in the integer domain. Apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-principal-maximal-dim-one-regular`, then transport by step 4.
6. The image subring of a field is nonzero and a domain, so its zero ideal is prime,
   giving a point of its spectrum. The whole spectrum is open and an affine
   neighbourhood of every point; steps 3 and 5 give its Noetherian regular coordinate
   ring. By `def-weil-affine-spectrum` and `def-weil-regular-scheme`, it is regular. -/
theorem range_generic_regular_model
    (L : Type u) [Field L] [Algebra ℚ L]
    [Module.Finite ℚ L] [IsPurelyInseparable ℚ L] :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap ℚ L) ∧
      Function.Bijective (algebraMap ℤ (Int.castRingHom L).range) ∧
      IsNoetherianRing (Int.castRingHom L).range ∧
      IsRegularRing (Int.castRingHom L).range ∧
      Module.Finite ℤ (Int.castRingHom L).range ∧
      Submodule.span ℤ ({1} : Set (Int.castRingHom L).range) = ⊤ ∧
      (∀ x : L, ∃ b c : (Int.castRingHom L).range,
        (c : L) ≠ 0 ∧ x = (b : L) / (c : L)) ∧
      Nonempty (AlgebraicGeometry.Spec (CommRingCat.of (Int.castRingHom L).range)) ∧
      AlgebraicGeometry.IsRegularScheme
        (AlgebraicGeometry.Spec (CommRingCat.of (Int.castRingHom L).range)) := by
  sorry

end Int
